\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Example}
\title{Terminal and process labels on the same four-step trace}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{feedback}
\tag{process-supervision}

\p{A four-step arithmetic trace can receive a correct terminal label while
retaining an incorrect intermediate process label.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  tracebox/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\small,
    text width=22mm, minimum height=12mm},
  flow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[tracebox] (s1) at (0,0) {$2+3=5$\\$f_1=1$};
\node[tracebox] (s2) at (2.8,0) {$5\cdot4=20$\\$f_2=1$};
\node[tracebox] (s3) at (5.6,0) {$20-1=18$\\$f_3=0$};
\node[tracebox] (s4) at (8.4,0) {correct to $19$\\$f_4=1$};
\draw[flow] (s1) -- (s2);
\draw[flow] (s2) -- (s3);
\draw[flow] (s3) -- (s4);
\node[font=\small] at (4.2,1.25) {terminal label $f_{\rm out}=1$};
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The final answer #{19} is correct, so outcome supervision supplies
#{f_{\rm out}=1}. The declared process labels are
#{(f_1,f_2,f_3,f_4)=(1,1,0,1)} and expose the corrected arithmetic error at
step three. The labels are attached to the displayed prefixes, which remain
unchanged by those annotations.}

\p{The two supervision regimes follow the outcome- and process-supervised
reward-model constructions in
\citet{the outcome- and process-supervised reward-model subsections}{lightman2023verify}.
The two observations are thereby separated. Noise-free labels and a general
policy advantage for process supervision are not consequences of the finite
trace.}
